{
  "root_cause": "The dropdown's dataApiKeydownHandler unconditionally prevents default and stops propagation for Escape key, preventing the modal from receiving the Escape event when the dropdown is not open.",
  "repair_strategy": "Modify dataApiKeydownHandler to allow Escape to bubble up when the dropdown is not showing (i.e., button lacks the SHOW class), while preserving existing behavior to close the dropdown when it is open.",
  "edits": [
    {
      "path": "js/src/dropdown.js",
      "sha256": "db8d2f4faa0a928bd1599a71441f38ca61ac3fcb69f4631bcdc74dc9edd5aa18",
      "before": "  static dataApiKeydownHandler(event) {
    // If not input/textarea:
    //  - And not a key in REGEXP_KEYDOWN => not a dropdown command
    // If input/textarea:
    //  - If space key => not a dropdown command
    //  - If key is other than escape
    //    - If key is not up or down => not a dropdown command
    //    - If trigger inside the menu => not a dropdown command
    if (/input|textarea/i.test(event.target.tagName) ?
      event.key === SPACE_KEY || (event.key !== ESCAPE_KEY &&
      ((event.key !== ARROW_DOWN_KEY && event.key !== ARROW_UP_KEY) ||
        event.target.closest(SELECTOR_MENU))) :
      !REGEXP_KEYDOWN.test(event.key)) {
      return
    }

    event.preventDefault()
    event.stopPropagation()

    if (isDisabled(this)) {
      return
    }

    const parent = Dropdown.getParentFromElement(this)
    const isActive = this.classList.contains(CLASS_NAME_SHOW)

    if (event.key === ESCAPE_KEY) {
      const button = this.matches(SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE) ? this : SelectorEngine.prev(this, SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE)[0]
      button.focus()
      Dropdown.clearMenus()
      return
    }

    if (!isActive && (event.key === ARROW_UP_KEY || event.key === ARROW_DOWN_KEY)) {
      const button = this.matches(SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE) ? this : SelectorEngine.prev(this, SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE)[0]
      button.click()
      return
    }

    if (!isActive || event.key === SPACE_KEY) {
      Dropdown.clearMenus()
      return
    }

    const items = SelectorEngine.find(SELECTOR_VISIBLE_ITEMS, parent).filter(isVisible)

    if (!items.length) {
      return
    }

    let index = items.indexOf(event.target)

    // Up
    if (event.key === ARROW_UP_KEY && index > 0) {
      index--
    }

    // Down
    if (event.key === ARROW_DOWN_KEY && index < items.length - 1) {
      index++
    }

    // index is -1 if the first keydown is an ArrowUp
    index = index === -1 ? 0 : index

    items[index].focus()
  }",
      "after": "  static dataApiKeydownHandler(event) {
    // If not input/textarea:
    //  - And not a key in REGEXP_KEYDOWN => not a dropdown command
    // If input/textarea:
    //  - If space key => not a dropdown command
    //  - If key is other than escape
    //    - If key is not up or down => not a dropdown command
    //    - If trigger inside the menu => not a dropdown command
    if (/input|textarea/i.test(event.target.tagName) ?
      event.key === SPACE_KEY || (event.key !== ESCAPE_KEY &&
      ((event.key !== ARROW_DOWN_KEY && event.key !== ARROW_UP_KEY) ||
        event.target.closest(SELECTOR_MENU))) :
      !REGEXP_KEYDOWN.test(event.key)) {
      return
    }

    if (event.key === ESCAPE_KEY) {
      const button = this.matches(SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE) ? this : SelectorEngine.prev(this, SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE)[0]
      if (!this.classList.contains(CLASS_NAME_SHOW)) {
        return // allow event to bubble to modal
      }
      button.focus()
      Dropdown.clearMenus()
    }

    event.preventDefault()
    event.stopPropagation()

    if (isDisabled(this)) {
      return
    }

    const parent = Dropdown.getParentFromElement(this)
    const isActive = this.classList.contains(CLASS_NAME_SHOW)

    if (!isActive && (event.key === ARROW_UP_KEY || event.key === ARROW_DOWN_KEY)) {
      const button = this.matches(SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE) ? this : SelectorEngine.prev(this, SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE)[0]
      button.click()
      return
    }

    if (!isActive || event.key === SPACE_KEY) {
      Dropdown.clearMenus()
      return
    }

    const items = SelectorEngine.find(SELECTOR_VISIBLE_ITEMS, parent).filter(isVisible)

    if (!items.length) {
      return
    }

    let index = items.indexOf(event.target)

    // Up
    if (event.key === ARROW_UP_KEY && index > 0) {
      index--
    }

    // Down
    if (event.key === ARROW_DOWN_KEY && index < items.length - 1) {
      index++
    }

    // index is -1 if the first keydown is an ArrowUp
    index = index === -1 ? 0 : index

    items[index].focus()
  }"
    }
  ],
  "expected_accessibility_effect": "Pressing Escape while focus is on the access button will now hide the share dialog, making the button invisible, allowing keyboard users to dismiss the dialog.",
  "functional_risks": [
    "Altered Escape handling may affect edge cases where dropdown state is ambiguous; however, the change only affects Escape when dropdown is not open, preserving existing behavior when dropdown is open.",
    "Ensure that other keyboard interactions (ArrowUp, ArrowDown, Space) remain unaffected."
  ],
  "verification_requirements": [
    "Verify that Escape closes the share dialog when the dropdown menu is not open.",
    "Verify that Escape closes only the dropdown menu when it is open, without closing the modal.",
    "Verify that ArrowUp, ArrowDown, and Space keys still operate correctly in both open and closed dropdown states."
  ]
}

reasoning:
We need to analyze the problem.

The journey:

Step sequence:
1. seek #open-share (maxTabs 24)
2. press Enter -> expects visible #access-toggle true
3. seek #access-toggle (maxTabs 3)
4. press Escape -> expects visible #access-toggle false

Observed failure: At step 4 pressing Escape does NOT cause #access-toggle to become invisible (still visible). Actually after pressing Escape, the observed shows #access-toggle still visible (the same as before), meaning Escape didn't close something.

Looking at observed state: After Escape press, the #access-toggle remains visible, with aria-expanded="false"? Wait the HTML snippet shows:

<button class="btn btn-outline-secondary dropdown-toggle" type="button" id="access-toggle" data-bs-toggle="dropdown" aria-expanded="false">Access: <span id="access-level">Only you</span></button>

So the button itself remained visible (it always is). But expectation was that after Escape, the #access-toggle becomes invisible? That seems odd: maybe they expect that pressing Escape when focus is on the access button should close the dropdown menu (which is inside the modal) and also perhaps close the modal? Let's examine the UI.

The modal (#share) contains a dropdown button (#access-toggle) that toggles a dropdown menu (class .dropdown-menu). When the dropdown is open, the button gets class "show" and aria-expanded="true". Pressing Escape should close the dropdown menu (i.e., hide the dropdown-menu, remove show class from button, set aria-expanded false). However the test expects that after Escape, the #access-toggle becomes invisible? Actually they assert visible false for #access-toggle. That suggests they think the button itself should be hidden? Or maybe they mis-specified: They might intend that the dropdown menu (which is not directly selectable) disappears, causing the button to lose focus? Hmm.

But looking at the observed after Escape: The button still visible, aria-expanded false (so dropdown is closed). So the dropdown is already closed? Wait before Escape, what was the state? Let's look at before Escape in observed: The before state shows same HTML with aria-expanded="false". So before pressing Escape, the dropdown was not expanded (aria-expanded false). That means the dropdown menu was not open. Then pressing Escape did nothing (still not open). The test expected that after Escape, the button becomes invisible (maybe they expected the modal to close?). Actually the modal is still open (share modal). The focus is on the access button inside the modal. Pressing Escape should close the modal (if keyboard is enabled). In the modal component, there is handling for Escape key to hide modal if keyboard:true. Indeed modal.js has _setEscapeEvent that listens for keydown.dismiss and if keyboard true and key Escape, calls hide().

Thus pressing Escape while focus is inside the modal should close the modal (hide it). The modal's hiding would cause the modal to be removed from view, and likely the button inside it would become invisible (since modal is hidden via display:none or removed). However the observed after Escape shows the button still visible and still in the DOM (since modal not hidden). So the Escape keypress was not processed by the modal to hide it. Why?

Look at observed keyEvents: we see a keydown for Escape with defaultPrevented:true, target #access-toggle, type keydown. So the Escape key was intercepted and default prevented. Something prevented the modal from receiving it? Perhaps the dropdown component's keydown handler prevented default and stopped propagation, thus preventing the modal from seeing it.

In dropdown.js, there is a dataApiKeydownHandler attached to document for EVENT_KEYDOWN_DATA_API on SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE and SELECTOR_MENU. This handler handles Escape key: if event.key === ESCAPE_KEY, then it focuses the button and calls Dropdown.clearMenus(). It also does event.preventDefault() and event.stopPropagation().

Thus when focus is on the #access-toggle button (which has data-bs-toggle="dropdown"), pressing Escape triggers the dropdown's dataApiKeydownHandler, which prevents default and stops propagation, so the modal's keydown listener (on _element for EVENT_KEYDOWN_DISMISS) does not receive the event (since propagation stopped). Therefore modal does not hide.

Goal: Escape should close the dialog (modal) while focus is on the access button. According to requirement, Escape inside the open access menu closes only the menu (but here the menu is not open). Actually regression guard says: "Escape inside the open access menu closes only the menu". There's a regression guard profile keyboard with actions: open-share, Enter, seek access-toggle, ArrowDown, Escape must hold: #access-toggle visible true and #shared-with text "Only you". That suggests they want Escape to close the dropdown menu (not the modal) when the dropdown menu is open. However the failing test is about Escape to dismiss the dialog when focus is on the access button (and dropdown not open). Wait the failure category REPLAY: Expected state did not occur after action step 3 (which is pressing Escape). The assertion: visible #access-toggle value false. They expected the button to become invisible after Escape. That suggests they think Escape should close the modal (making the button invisible). But the regression guard expects Escape inside the open access menu closes only the menu (i.e., when dropdown open, Escape closes menu but not modal). So there are two contexts: when dropdown is closed, Escape should close modal; when dropdown is open, Escape should close dropdown only.

Thus we need to adjust the dropdown's keydown handler to not prevent propagation when the dropdown menu is not showing (i.e., when the button is not expanded). Currently, the handler prevents default and stops propagation for Escape regardless of state. We should modify it so that if the dropdown is not showing (i.e., !this._element.classList.contains(CLASS_NAME_SHOW)), we allow the event to propagate (i.e., not call preventDefault/stopPropagation?) Actually we still want to hide the dropdown menu if open, but if not open we should let the event bubble up to modal.

In the dataApiKeydownHandler (static method), they check if event.key === ESCAPE_KEY then:

const button = this.matches(SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE) ? this : SelectorEngine.prev(this, SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE)[0];
button.focus();
Dropdown.clearMenus();
return;

They call Dropdown.clearMenus() which hides any open dropdown menus (including possibly this one). They also preventDefault and stopPropagation earlier in the function (at top: event.preventDefault(); event.stopPropagation();). Actually look at the start of dataApiKeydownHandler:

if (/input|textarea/i.test(event.target.tagName) ? ... : !REGEXP_KEYDOWN.test(event.key)) {
  return;
}
event.preventDefault()
event.stopPropagation()

Thus for Escape key, they always preventDefault and stopPropagation, then handle.

We need to change logic: For Escape key, if the dropdown is not showing (i.e., button not having SHOW class), we should not preventDefault/stopPropagation, allowing modal to handle it. If dropdown is showing, we should keep current behavior (preventDefault, stopPropagation, clearMenus, focus button).

